    "description": "Orbital Tactics runs a fixed-step simulation where Bullet, Missile, Drone, Hull, Shield, and Asteroid objects can collide. CollisionResolver receives two independently selected participants and must choose the result: a missile striking a shield may deactivate the shield, a drone touching a hull may apply impact damage, and a bullet meeting an asteroid may ricochet. Some combinations have no interaction. The rules team owns the entity classes, while the physics team owns effect calculations, and either side may add concrete entity kinds over time. CollisionResolver must not contain a growing ladder of concrete-type checks, and callers that collect candidate pairs must not know the applicable interaction rule. At runtime, selection must account for both participants' concrete types, not merely the first argument or a numeric category attached by the caller. The engine also needs to register and log each pair-specific handler so that an unsupported combination can be reported explicitly rather than silently treated as a generic collision.",
    "decision_statement": "Whether interaction selection should be resolved from the runtime types of both participants, with pair-specific rules kept out of the collision caller.",
    "notes": "Double-dispatch fits selecting pair behavior from both participants runtime types without caller type ladders; dispatch-table is the near-miss (pair-keyed handler lookup) and visitor supports the two-axis dispatch reading."
  },
